{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He called to Moses.</b> Since it said above at the end of the book, ״וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן״ and Moses could not enter the Tent of Meeting etc., it was necessary to call him and give him permission to enter. Therefore it wrote, ״And He called to Moses.״ And so the interpretation is: the glory of HaShem mentioned above called to Moses from the Tent of Meeting and spoke to him. And so we find in Targum Yerushalmi: And it was, when Moses had completed before Him the Tabernacle and erected it and sanctified it and all its vessels, Moses pondered in his heart and said: Mount Sinai, whose manifestation was a temporary manifestation and whose sanctity was a temporary sanctity — I did not ascend into it until the time it was said to me from before HaShem; how much more so this Tabernacle, whose manifestation is an eternal manifestation and whose sanctity is an eternal sanctity — is it not so that I should not enter it until the time it is said to me from before HaShem? And the word of HaShem came to Moses etc. And when he was inside the Tent, he would hear the voice coming to him from between the two cherubim, as it is written at the end of parashat Naso." "<b>To him.</b> Rashi explained: could it be that they would hear the voice of the calling? Scripture says, ״קול לו״ — a voice to him. That is, in parashat Naso it is written ״וישמע את הקול מדבר אליו״ — one might have said ״מדבר לו״, and it says ״מדבר אליו״ to teach you that to him alone the voice was drawn. Thus Rashi explained at the beginning of Yoma (4a)." "<b>Saying.</b> It is a redundant expression appended to ״and He spoke,״ as he explained in parashat Noah, similarly."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:36.793788968Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}